  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nEarly this morning, high pressure over northwestern Ontario is \nkeeping our weather generally dry and cool. Between this high \npressure and lower pressure to our south there is a relatively \ntight pressure gradient, which is maintaining breezy northeast \nwinds, particularly near Lake Superior. Drier air is filtering \ninto the area at low levels, but mid and high clouds have spread\nacross the area from the low pressure system to the south. \nTemperatures dropping to overnight lows into the mid 30s \nprompted a Frost Advisory through 8 AM for Koochiching, North \nSt. Louis, and northern Cook and Lake counties. Today, we expect\nthe northeast winds to slowly decrease as the gradient weakens,\nand clouds will gradually clear from northwest to southeast \nduring the daytime. \n\nTonight into Tuesday morning is likely to be our coldest night \nof the week. With the Canadian high settling nearly overhead, \nmostly clear or clear skies and light winds will set the stage \nfor excellent radiational cooling. Lows are forecast to plummet \ninto the 30s for most inland areas, while lakeshore communities \nremain slightly warmer in the 40s. A widespread frost is highly \nlikely across the Northland, and some locations north of the \nIron Range could see temperatures dip below freezing. Additional\nfrost or freeze headlines will likely be needed for tonight \ninto Tuesday morning. \n\nOn Tuesday, the high pressure center will begin drifting toward\nthe east, causing our winds to veer southeasterly. This return \nflow marks the beginning of a gradual warming trend that will \ncarry us through the remainder of the week. Afternoon highs on \nTuesday will rebound comfortably into the 60s, though the \nonshore flow will keep the North Shore a bit cooler in the upper\n50s. Another chilly night is expected Tuesday night, bringing \nyet another chance for patchy frost inland.\n\nUpper level ridging will build across the Upper Midwest for the\nmiddle and later part of the work week. A shortwave riding over\nthe top of the ridge brings a chance for showers Wednesday \nnight into Thursday, but dry low levels will limit widespread \nrainfall. Otherwise, the ridging aloft will provide generally \ndry and quiet weather with temperatures slowly rising into the \nweekend. Highs will reach the mid 60s to lower 70s for Friday \nand Saturday. Precipitation chances remain quite low and spotty \nheading into the weekend even as the ridging eventually slides \neastward.",
